<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chinsurah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Chinsurah is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hooghly-river">Hooghly River</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bengal">West Bengal</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, about 35 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolkata">Kolkata</a>. From the 17th century it was the main base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a>, trading in textiles, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, saltpetre and opium. A British victory there in 1759 ended <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> ambitions in the region, and the town passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-dutch-treaty-of-1824">Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chinsurah.jpg" alt="Chinsurah" width="320" height="202" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mukut Tapadar · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clock_Tower_of_Chinsurah_in_Hooghly_district.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
